    "https://nwmissouri.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Northwest-Missouri-State-University-Maryville/Kansas-City-Regional-Recruitment-Coordinator_R-00198": {
      "title": "Kansas City Regional Recruitment Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSALARY: $20.37 POSITION TITLE: Kansas City Regional Recruitment Coordinator DEPARTMENT : Admissions Recruitment JOB DESCRIPTION: EMPLOYMENT TERMS: Full-time, 12-month PRIMARY DUTY: The Kansas City Regional Recruitment Coordinator must have a passion for Northwest Missouri State University, be highly motivated, and goal-oriented to attract, recruit, and retain prospective students to meet the enrollment goals at Northwest. The desired candidate will reside in the KC metro area. Recruitment Coordinators must represent and promote Northwest Missouri State University accurately and persuasively to prospective students, parents, guidance counselors, educators and other publics within the assigned territory. Additionally, the Recruitment Coordinator assists the Admissions-Recruitment team with the administration of all campus recruitment activities.   ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Responsible for the coordination of recruitment and yield in Kansas City metro area. This remote position will be based within the recruiting territory to attend off-campus recruitment activities (including but not limited to counselor luncheons, high school visits, college fairs, regional conferences, award presentations, and more). Travels to Maryville for on-campus events and training as necessary. Collaborate and work closely with Great Jobs KC-KC Scholars partnership to build and maintain positive relationships with Great Jobs KC Advocates, partner high schools, and high school counselors. Assist with recruitment activities and college fairs at area community colleges to promote Northwest to prospective transfer students. Responsible for the coordination and management of all virtual recruitment activities including but not limited to large group, small group, and personalized sessions with their assigned territory. Create and maintain positive relationships with prospective students, parents, guidance counselors, educators, and other publics within assigned territory by providing information on academics, value, scholarships, housing, student life, and general university activities Analyze student data to select which recruitment strategies and information is most effective for each prospective student. Utilize data management to target select populations for all armchair recruitment activities. Actively conduct armchair recruitment that occurs during all stages of the recruitment funnel. Includes contact via emails, personal notes, phone calls, texting, and social media. Review, update, and utilize computer-based lead tracking systems to aid in prospective student recruitment Utilize value-added and relationship-based techniques when working with prospective students, parents, guidance counselors, educators, and other publics Create and achieve territorial goals and objectives based on trend data, historical reporting, and evaluation of prospect and admitted database information Analyze, read, and report feedback to Admissions-Recruitment leadership with on-going competitive environment regarding Agricultural Sciences Assist with the coordination of Summer Orientation and Registration, Advantage week, and General Registration Survey residents of assigned territory to assess needs of prospective students, parents, guidance counselors, educators, and other publics Serve as a liaison between various internal departments in resolution of prospective students, parents, guidance counselors, educators, and other publics concerns and issues Performs other duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Undergraduate Degree Certification/License: Driver’s license Experience: 1-2 years of experience in the field or a related area Skills: Extensive knowledge of colleges and universities; ability to communicate effectively with people from diverse backgrounds as well as cultivate awareness of cultural diversity; strong organizational skills; strong public speaking skills; ability to load, unload, and set up exhibits. Proficiency in Microsoft Office programs and high comfort level with technology and the ability to analyze and present data effectively. WORKING CONDITIONS: This position will work in a remote office environment with a work schedule determined based on service needs, which will likely include early morning, night, and/or weekend hours. TYPICAL PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Ability to lift up to 20 pounds COMMUNITY: Northwest Missouri State University located in Maryville, Missouri, a community of 12,000 with a diverse economy and a strong industrial base. Maryville is located 45 miles north of St. Joseph, Missouri, 90 miles north of Kansas City, Missouri, and 110 miles southeast of Omaha, Nebraska. For more information visit the city’s website at www.maryville.org . UNIVERSITY: With an enrollment of more than 8,500 students, Northwest is a coeducational, primarily residential four-year university that offers a broad range of undergraduate and selected graduate programs on its Maryville campus and through Northwest Online .   Founded in 1905, Northwest boasts a high retention rate and a graduation rate in the 95th percentile among its peers. Additionally, results of the Ruffalo Noel Levitz Student Satisfaction Inventory show Northwest students are more satisfied than students at national peers, and 78 percent of Northwest students report they would repeat their university experience, compared to 75 percent of students surveyed nationally at their respective institutions.   Furthermore, the University is a national model for student success and career placement with 93 percent of bachelor’s degree earners and 98 percent of master’s degree earners securing employment or continuing their education within six months of completing a degree at Northwest, according to the most recent data .    Northwest places a high emphasis on profession-based learning to help graduates get a jumpstart on their careers while maintaining competitive tuition rates and generous financial assistance to help minimize the financial barriers students may face when pursuing a college degree.   Students have opportunities to build their résumés with experiences on campus in nearly every area of study, including the  Horace Mann Laboratory School ,  National Public Radio affiliate KXCV , the R.T. Wright Farm ,  Mozingo Outdoor Education Recreation Area ,  a partnership with the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency or  Knacktive , a student-driven integrated digital marketing communications agency. In conjunction with its emergency and disaster management program, Northwest organizes and hosts Missouri Hope , an annual mass casualty training exercise that attracts first responders and emergency workers from throughout the nation and provides hands-on training in preparation for a natural disaster.   The University’s vibrant and diverse learning community also offers more than 150 student organizations, and  textbooks and a laptop are included  in tuition, saving students an estimated $6,900 over four years. Northwest offers 1,200 student employment positions, allowing students to build professional skills through its internationally benchmarked student employment program.    For more information about Northwest and its performance, visit  www.nwmissouri.edu/aboutus/facts/ ."
    },
    "https://nwmissouri.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Northwest-Missouri-State-University-Maryville/Upward-Bound-Math-Science-Project-Director_R-00193-1": {
      "title": "Upward Bound Math/Science Project Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSALARY: $48,890.00 POSITION TITLE: Upward Bound Math/Science Project Director DEPARTMENT : TRIO JOB DESCRIPTION: EMPLOYMENT TERMS: Full-time, 12 months PRIMARY DUTY: The primary duties of this position include providing leadership and oversight for Northwest’s Upward Bound Math and Science (UBMS) program to ensure effective program operations, student engagement and success, and compliance with federal TRIO regulations and grant objectives. UBMS supports 71 high school students from Kansas, Missouri, Iowa, and Nebraska who demonstrate academic need and are first-generation and/or low-income. Responsibilities include program operations, student recruitment and success, academic and STEM programming, staff supervision, grant compliance, data and reporting, and coordination of academic-year and summer residential programming. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Provide leadership for the Upward Bound Math and Science (UBMS) program to ensure strong student engagement, effective program services, and progress toward program outcomes. Identify and actively recruit the required number of eligible high school students to participate in the grant project each year. Design and implement an engaging, STEM-focused academic curriculum for the UBMS summer residential program that promotes academic preparation, college readiness, career exploration, and student success. Hire, train, and supervise UBMS professional staff, academic staff, and student assistants for the summer residential programs.  Oversee Academic Year school-based mentors and academic year STEM projects and activities.  Assist the Coordinator of Counseling in designing and implementing a positive residential and/or virtual programming experience for the students. Develop and maintain program marketing materials and promotional content. Maintain Project Database and ensure timely, accurate submission of compliance reports to the Department of Education, including documentation of student participation and budget expenditures, in full accordance with federal requirements. Work with the TRIO Specialist to develop and manage the program budget to ensure compliant, effective use of federal funds. Monitor expenditures, coordinate procurement, prepare financial documentation, and maintain financial records in accordance with federal and institutional requirements.  Assist with developing and submitting grant proposals to secure continued program funding. Other duties as assigned. REQUIREMENTS: Education:  Master’s degree in Education, Student Development, Counseling or Administration. Experience:  Minimum three years of experience working with disadvantaged students or students in a high school aged educational setting.  Experience required with designing challenging academic curriculums, assessing student needs and supervising others. WORKING CONDITIONS: This position will work in an office style setting from the hours of 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with an hour lunch break and occasional nights and weekends. Some conference and program travel is required. Attendance at eight Saturday Academies is also required. This position is identified as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). The CSA will attend required CSA training and is an official of the University who has significant responsibility for students and campus activities and the authority to take action for the University.         SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: Supervise Coordinator of Counseling, seven Field Tutors during academic year, and 15-20 part time employees during the summer component of the program EQUIPMENT/MATERIALS USED: Computer and presentation materials TYPICAL PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Ability to lift up to 20 pounds COMMUNITY: Northwest Missouri State University located in Maryville, Missouri, a community of 12,000 with a diverse economy and a strong industrial base. Maryville is located 45 miles north of St. Joseph, Missouri, 90 miles north of Kansas City, Missouri, and 110 miles southeast of Omaha, Nebraska. For more information visit the city’s website at www.maryville.org . UNIVERSITY: With an enrollment of more than 8,500 students, Northwest is a coeducational, primarily residential four-year university that offers a broad range of undergraduate and selected graduate programs on its Maryville campus and through Northwest Online .   Founded in 1905, Northwest boasts a high retention rate and a graduation rate in the 95th percentile among its peers. Additionally, results of the Ruffalo Noel Levitz Student Satisfaction Inventory show Northwest students are more satisfied than students at national peers, and 78 percent of Northwest students report they would repeat their university experience, compared to 75 percent of students surveyed nationally at their respective institutions.   Furthermore, the University is a national model for student success and career placement with 93 percent of bachelor’s degree earners and 98 percent of master’s degree earners securing employment or continuing their education within six months of completing a degree at Northwest, according to the most recent data .    Northwest places a high emphasis on profession-based learning to help graduates get a jumpstart on their careers while maintaining competitive tuition rates and generous financial assistance to help minimize the financial barriers students may face when pursuing a college degree.   Students have opportunities to build their résumés with experiences on campus in nearly every area of study, including the  Horace Mann Laboratory School ,  National Public Radio affiliate KXCV , the R.T. Wright Farm ,  Mozingo Outdoor Education Recreation Area ,  a partnership with the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency or  Knacktive , a student-driven integrated digital marketing communications agency. In conjunction with its emergency and disaster management program, Northwest organizes and hosts Missouri Hope , an annual mass casualty training exercise that attracts first responders and emergency workers from throughout the nation and provides hands-on training in preparation for a natural disaster.   The University’s vibrant and diverse learning community also offers more than 150 student organizations, and  textbooks and a laptop are included  in tuition, saving students an estimated $6,900 over four years. Northwest offers 1,200 student employment positions, allowing students to build professional skills through its internationally benchmarked student employment program.    For more information about Northwest and its performance, visit  www.nwmissouri.edu/aboutus/facts/ ."
    },
    "https://nwmissouri.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Northwest-Missouri-State-University-Maryville/Wellness-Educator_R-00183": {
      "title": "Wellness Educator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSALARY: $39,740.00 POSITION TITLE: Wellness Educator DEPARTMENT : Wellness Education and Prevention JOB DESCRIPTION: EMPLOYMENT TERMS: Full-time, ten-month PRIMARY DUTY: Wellness Educator is responsible for the coordination of university wellness programs through Wellness Services under the direction of the Director of Health Promotion and Prevention. This position works to mobilize, create, and sustain campus-wide health promotion practices to create an environment that promotes student success and personal development. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Student Engagement and Health Education Lead assigned health promotion and prevention programs, presentations, trainings, outreach, and student engagement initiatives. Develop and sustain an effective Peer Education program, including recruitment, training, supervision, and ongoing development of student participants. Serve as a departmental resource for assigned health promotion and prevention areas and apply evidence-informed practices to meet student and community needs. Collaborate with campus and community partners to promote health education, wellness, and student engagement. Lead communication, marketing, and social media efforts for assigned Health Promotion and Prevention initiatives. BASICS (Brief Alcohol Screening and Intervention for College Students) Program Provide BASICS training and individual intervention sessions for identified students using evidence-informed practices and motivational interviewing strategies. Coordinate BASICS program operations, documentation, follow-up, and assessment to support effective student services. Prevention Coalitions and Program Coordination Serve as the day-to-day coordinator and facilitator for Maryville Partners in Prevention (MVPIP) and participate in Missouri Partners in Prevention (PIP). Coordinate coalition activities, communication, reporting, and implementation of assigned prevention priorities. Coordinate administration of the Missouri Assessment of College Health Behaviors (MACHB) and utilize findings to inform prevention programming. Develop and implement assigned prevention initiatives aligned with university priorities, student needs, and evidence-informed practices. Program Operations, Assessment, and Continuous Improvement Lead the planning, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of assigned health promotion and prevention programs, initiatives, and events. Independently manage assigned projects from development through implementation and evaluation, including coordination of logistics, partnerships, communication, and resources. Collect and utilize program data, participant feedback, and assessment findings to evaluate effectiveness and inform program improvements. Identify emerging needs and opportunities and provide recommendations to the Director regarding assigned programs and initiatives. Professional and Departmental Responsibilities Independently manage assigned programs and projects, exercising appropriate judgment and decision-making within established departmental priorities and policies. Maintain effective relationships with campus and community partners and collaborate with Wellness Services staff to support departmental initiatives. Provide regular updates and recommendations to the Director regarding assigned programs, emerging needs, and opportunities for improvement. Participate in departmental planning, professional development, and collaborative initiatives as assigned. Other duties as assigned. REQUIREMENTS: Education: Bachelor’s Degree in Nursing, Public Health, Recreation, Psychology, Health Education or related field Skills:  Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to lead, supervise and motivate others, ease in public speaking and commitment to excellence in all areas of education and provision of services, ability to build and sustain effective collaboration with university and community partners. WORKING CONDITIONS: Work will take place in multiple campus and community sites with varying temperatures and accommodations; working some evenings/weekends are required; some travel is required. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES: This position will potentially supervise graduate assistants, student employees, and student peer educators. TYPICAL PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Lifting of boxes and materials of up to 50 pounds COMMUNITY: Northwest Missouri State University located in Maryville, Missouri, a community of 12,000 with a diverse economy and a strong industrial base. Maryville is located 45 miles north of St. Joseph, Missouri, 90 miles north of Kansas City, Missouri, and 110 miles southeast of Omaha, Nebraska. For more information visit the city’s website at www.maryville.org . UNIVERSITY: With an enrollment of more than 8,500 students, Northwest is a coeducational, primarily residential four-year university that offers a broad range of undergraduate and selected graduate programs on its Maryville campus and through Northwest Online .   Founded in 1905, Northwest boasts a high retention rate and a graduation rate in the 95th percentile among its peers. Additionally, results of the Ruffalo Noel Levitz Student Satisfaction Inventory show Northwest students are more satisfied than students at national peers, and 78 percent of Northwest students report they would repeat their university experience, compared to 75 percent of students surveyed nationally at their respective institutions.   Furthermore, the University is a national model for student success and career placement with 93 percent of bachelor’s degree earners and 98 percent of master’s degree earners securing employment or continuing their education within six months of completing a degree at Northwest, according to the most recent data .    Northwest places a high emphasis on profession-based learning to help graduates get a jumpstart on their careers while maintaining competitive tuition rates and generous financial assistance to help minimize the financial barriers students may face when pursuing a college degree.   Students have opportunities to build their résumés with experiences on campus in nearly every area of study, including the  Horace Mann Laboratory School ,  National Public Radio affiliate KXCV , the R.T. Wright Farm ,  Mozingo Outdoor Education Recreation Area ,  a partnership with the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency or  Knacktive , a student-driven integrated digital marketing communications agency. In conjunction with its emergency and disaster management program, Northwest organizes and hosts Missouri Hope , an annual mass casualty training exercise that attracts first responders and emergency workers from throughout the nation and provides hands-on training in preparation for a natural disaster.   The University’s vibrant and diverse learning community also offers more than 150 student organizations, and  textbooks and a laptop are included  in tuition, saving students an estimated $6,900 over four years. Northwest offers 1,200 student employment positions, allowing students to build professional skills through its internationally benchmarked student employment program.    For more information about Northwest and its performance, visit  www.nwmissouri.edu/aboutus/facts/ ."
